  <span class=sf-dump-index>2</span> => <span class=sf-dump-note>array:22</span> [<samp data-depth=2 class=sf-dump-compact>
    \"<span class=sf-dump-key>id</span>\" => <span class=sf-dump-num>1536</span>
    \"<span class=sf-dump-key>name</span>\" => \"<span class=sf-dump-str title=\"97 characters\">Paratextual Framing and Language Ideology: the Translation of Japanese Literature in Maoist China</span>\"
    \"<span class=sf-dump-key>slug</span>\" => \"<span class=sf-dump-str title=\"96 characters\">Paratextual-Framing-and-Language-Ideology-the-Translation-of-Japanese-Literature-in-Maoist-China</span>\"
    \"<span class=sf-dump-key>aname</span>\" => \"<span class=sf-dump-str title=\"8 characters\">Fan Xie*</span>\"
    \"<span class=sf-dump-key>abstract</span>\" => \"\"\"
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">&lt;p class=&quot;MsoNormal&quot; style=&quot;line-height: 115%; font-family: Calibri, &amp;quot;sans-serif&amp;quot;; margin: 8px 0in 0px; text-align: justify; font-size: 15px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 16px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;serif&amp;quot;; color: black;&quot;&gt;This study examines<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        how Japanese literature was made readable and circulable in Maoist China<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        through paratextual framing. Drawing on publication notes, prefaces,<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        translators&#8217; afterwords, editors&#8217; notes, critical commentaries, circulation<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        labels, and materials from the translated literature journal &lt;em&gt;Zhaiyi&lt;/em&gt;, it argues that paratexts were<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        not peripheral additions to translated works, but a central mechanism through<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        which foreign literature was interpreted and managed. The analysis shows that<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        these paratexts worked through repeatable routines of classification, warning,<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        circulation control, and attribution. They classified authors and works before<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        reading, warned against politically inappropriate interpretations, limited<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        readership through internal circulation, and weakened individual translator<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        identity through collective, pen-name, or absent attribution. In the case of<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        Japanese literature, paratexts also produced a manageable image of Japan by<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        narrating Japanese society as a site of capitalist crisis, framing literary<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        value through a worker-peasant-soldier subject position, and arranging cultural<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        knowledge through lists and columns. The article shows that translation during<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        the Cultural Revolution was a language-ideological and cultural-political<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        practice, through which foreign texts were turned into controlled objects of<span class=\"sf-dump-default sf-dump-ns\">\\r\\n</span></span>
      <span class=sf-dump-str title=\"1949 characters\">        knowledge within a supervised system of publication and reading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;serif&amp;quot;;&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</span>
      \"\"\"
